0000                             ; =============================================================================
0000                             ;  B8Script interpreter for the 6502 (NMOS opcodes only), with the data stack
0000                             ;  in zero page
0000                             ;  Spec: docs/b8script.md (2026-10-09) plus the 2026-11-20 changes:
0000                             ;        [x stores in a variable, "[ " pushes an anonymous block, any other
0000                             ;        character after [ is an error, the heap pointer lives at z10+.
0000                             ;        The string escape is "/" (it was ">").
0000                             ;        Dividing by zero is not checked. -n? does no more editing than
0000                             ;        Backspace.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  This is the smaller of the two 6502 interpreters. b8s-6502.asm keeps the
0000                             ;  data stack in pages 2 and 3 (252 cells) and leaves zero page almost free;
0000                             ;  this one keeps it in zero page (112 cells), where the 6502's short
0000                             ;  addressing modes make nearly every handler smaller. The language is the
0000                             ;  same; the memory map and the machine-code contract differ.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  One switch, set in the EQU line below:
0000                             ;    CHECKED  0 = no checks (spec: "B8Script checks nothing")
0000                             ;             1 = stops on stack overrun, Ctrl-C, heap past the limit, a store
0000                             ;                 outside the program's own space, and a bad character after [,
0000                             ;                 printing a letter and the place, like  S @ 245
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Assembler: asm80, C6502 mode.
0000                             ; =============================================================================
0000                CHECKED:   EQU   0   ; 1 = checked build
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                             ;  Registers and zero page
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;    IP      zero-page pointer to the next program character (16 bits)
0000                             ;    X       the data-stack pointer. Every cell is two bytes in zero page, low
0000                             ;            byte first: the top cell (TOS) is 0,X and 1,X, the one under it
0000                             ;            (NOS) 2,X and 3,X. The stack grows down: a push is DEX DEX, a
0000                             ;            drop INX INX. A cell that holds an address is used where it is,
0000                             ;            with (0,X): nothing is copied to a pointer first.
0000                             ;    $FE,X   the two bytes under the top cell (zero-page indexing wraps, so
0000                             ;    $FF,X   this is -2,X and -1,X): free memory, used as scratch.
0000                             ;    S       the hardware stack is the RETURN stack: a word call or "(" pushes
0000                             ;            the reading position (high byte first, then low) and "]" or ")"
0000                             ;            pulls it. 100 entries (200 bytes) fit with room to spare.
0000                             ;    Y, A    scratch. Y is 0 after peek/getc.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Dispatch: step is called with JSR, so a handler ends with a plain RTS and
0000                             ;  lands on "JMP next" in the loop. Handlers that use the return stack ( ( ) \
0000                             ;  ] 00bbIA== and word calls ) first pull the 2-byte JSR frame off (PLA PLA) and end
0000                             ;  with JMP next instead.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  PTAB holds one byte per punctuation character: the low byte of its
0000                             ;  handler's address, less 1 (the dispatcher pushes an address and "returns"
0000                             ;  to it). The high byte comes from the character: the handlers of the first
0000                             ;  N1 characters start in the image's first page, those of the characters from
0000                             ;  N1 up to N2 in its second, and the rest in its third. The layout below
0000                             ;  keeps to that, and testing/chktab6502.py checks it in every build.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Every punctuation handler starts with the carry clear (the dispatcher
0000                             ;  leaves it so). Several of them count on that, where the comments say.
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  Machine-code contract (the grave): the code at TOS is entered as a
0000                             ;  subroutine, by JMP (T0) with the loop's return address on the hardware
0000                             ;  stack, so it ends with RTS (60h, which the hex loader appends). On entry the
0000                             ;  address has been dropped and X is the data-stack pointer: TOS is 0,X (low)
0000                             ;  and 1,X (high), NOS is 2,X and 3,X. To push: DEX, DEX, then store at 0,X
0000                             ;  and 1,X. To drop: INX, INX. The code may change A and Y, and X as the
0000                             ;  stack pointer; it must keep IP ($00/$01) and leave the hardware stack
0000                             ;  balanced. Zero page $02, $03, $07 and $0A-$0D are free for its own use.
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                IP:       EQU   00h   ; 00-01 reading position
0000                T0:       EQU   04h   ; scratch
0000                T1:       EQU   05h   
0000                T2:       EQU   06h   
0000                D0:       EQU   08h   ; divisor (division, number printing)
0000                D1:       EQU   09h   
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                             ;  Memory map
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;    0000-000F  the variables above, and scratch
0000                             ;    0010-00EF  the data stack: 112 cells. X = F0 when it is empty. (0E and
0000                             ;               0F are the scratch under the top cell when it is full.)
0000                             ;    0100-01FF  hardware stack = return stack, S starts at FF
0000                             ;    0400       LET  letter cells: A..Z at +00..+33, a..z at +40..+73 (a word
0000                             ;               cell is 64 bytes below its variable), heap pointer at +7C
0000                             ;               (= z10+), KEYBUF at +34, the -n? line buffer at +80..+FF
0000                             ;    0500       PROG the program text, NUL-terminated; the heap follows
0000                             ;    E000       the interpreter (ROM)
0000                             ; 
0000                             ;  The stacks sit below the letter page, so "the program's own space" (what
0000                             ;  the checked build lets ! and . write) is the page range LETPAGE..HEAPLIM-1.
0000                             ; -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
0000                KEY:      EQU   0FF06h   ; a waiting key in A, or A = 0 if none is
0000                             ;   waiting. The Z flag must match A (it does
0000                             ;   if the routine ends with LDA / RTS).
0000                CONOUT:   EQU   0FF03h   ; print the character in A
0000                EXIT:     EQU   0FF09h   ; where to go when the program ends
0000                             ;   KEY and CONOUT must preserve X and Y.
0000                DSE:      EQU   0F0h   ; X when the data stack is empty
0000                DSFULL:   EQU   10h   ; X when it is full
0000                LETPAGE:   EQU   04h   
0000                LET:      EQU   LETPAGE*256   
0000                PROG:     EQU   0500h   ; where the loader puts the program text
0000                PROG1:    EQU   PROG+1   
0000                HEAPLIM:   EQU   0E0h   ; the heap must stay below E000h (a page)
0000                HEAP:     EQU   LET+7Ch   ; z10+, the heap pointer
0000                KEYBUF:   EQU   LET+34h   ; a key held back by the break check
0000                LINEBUF:   EQU   LET+80h   ; -n? text, ending in CR
0000                RSLIM:    EQU   20h   ; lowest S allowed (checked build)
0000                MSLOOP:   EQU   40   ; n? inner loop count for about 1 ms; tune it
0000                BASE:     EQU   0E000h   ; where the image starts: a multiple of 512
0000                HALFPG:   EQU   BASE/512   ; (the dispatcher doubles this to get a page)
0000                N1:       EQU   8   ; ! " # $ % & ' (  start in the first page,
0000                N2:       EQU   21   ; ) * + , - . / : ; < = > ?  in the second,
0000                             ; @ [ \ ] ^ _ ` 00bbIHwg ~  in the third
0000                             ; udiv: unsigned TOS / D (D <= 8000h). TOS becomes the quotient; the
0000                             ; remainder is left in the scratch under it, at $FE,X and $FF,X.
0000                             ; Dividing by 2 is one shift (the spec wants that fast).
0000                             ; (A macro, because the two builds need it in different places: the
0000                             ; unchecked build uses it to fill the first page.)
0000                .macro UDIV,
0000                             ; 
0000                udiv:   LDA     #0
0000                 STA     0FEh,X
0000                 STA     0FFh,X
0000                 LDA     D0
0000                 EOR     #2
0000                 ORA     D1
0000                 BNE     ud0
0000                 LSR     1,X
0000                 ROR     0,X             ; the quotient
0000                 ROL     0FEh,X          ; the remainder: the bit that fell out
0000                 RTS
0000                ud0:    LDY     #16
0000                ud1:    ASL     0,X
0000                 ROL     1,X
0000                 ROL     0FEh,X
0000                 ROL     0FFh,X
0000                 SEC
0000                 LDA     0FEh,X
0000                 SBC     D0
0000                 STA     T0
0000                 LDA     0FFh,X
0000                 SBC     D1
0000                 BCC     ud2
0000                 STA     0FFh,X
0000                 LDA     T0
0000                 STA     0FEh,X
0000                 INC     0,X             ; the quotient bit (bit 0 is free)
0000                ud2:    DEY
0000                 BNE     ud1
0000                 RTS
0000                .endm
0000                 
E000                          .ORG   BASE   
E000                             ; =============================================================================
E000                             ;  Page 1 of 3. Startup: find the NUL, the heap starts after it
E000                             ; =============================================================================
E000   A2 FF        START:    LDX   #0FFh   
E002   9A                     TXS      ; return stack empty
E003   20 EE E3               JSR   rstip   
E006   20 DD E3     ST1:      JSR   getc   
E009   D0 FB                  BNE   st1   ; IP ends just past the NUL
E00B                          IF   CHECKED   
                              STA   KEYBUF   ; (A = 0) no key held back
E00B                          ENDIF      
E00B   A5 00                  LDA   IP   
E00D   8D 7C 04               STA   HEAP   
E010   A5 01                  LDA   IP+1   
E012   8D 7D 04               STA   HEAP+1   
E015   20 EE E3               JSR   rstip   
E018   A2 F0                  LDX   #DSE   
E01A                             ; ---- the inner loop ---------------------------------------------------------
E01A                NEXT:        
E01A                          IF   CHECKED   
                                 ; Both stacks are checked once per character. No handler moves X by
                                 ; more than 4, so X is caught before it wraps; an overrun by a cell
                                 ; or two writes on the scratch bytes below the stack. The return
                                 ; stack has overrun when S is below RSLIM (an underflow wraps S to
                                 ; 00-03).
                              CPX   #DSE+1   
                              BCS   e_stack   
                              CPX   #DSFULL   
                              BCC   e_stack   
                              TXA      ; TSX trashes X: keep it in A
                              TSX      
                              CPX   #RSLIM   
                              TAX      ; TAX leaves the carry alone
                              BCC   e_rstack   
E01A                          ENDIF      
E01A   20 0F E3               JSR   step   
E01D   4C 1A E0               JMP   next   
E020                          IF   CHECKED   
                    E_STACK:   LDA   #"S"   ; data stack over or under
                              DB   2Ch   ; (BIT abs: hides the next instruction)
                    E_RSTACK:   LDA   #"R"   ; return stack over or under
                              JMP   error   
E020                          ENDIF      
E020                             ; ---- handlers of ! " # $ % & ' ( : they must start in this page -------------
E020                             ; "$" prints the string at TOS, decoding escapes: "/" plus a character
E020                             ; clears that character"s bit 6 (/M is CR, /` is space; "/" and ""' have no
E020                             ; bit 6, so // and /" give themselves).
E020   20 9F E2     H_PRINT:   JSR   tosget   
E023   C9 22                  CMP   #22h   
E025   F0 14                  BEQ   pdrop   
E027   C9 2F                  CMP   #"/"   
E029   D0 05                  BNE   pr1   
E02B   20 9F E2               JSR   tosget   
E02E   29 BF                  AND   #0BFh   
E030   20 03 FF     PR1:      JSR   CONOUT   
E033   4C 20 E0               JMP   h_print   
E036   B5 00        H_EMIT:   LDA   0,X   ; "'"
E038   20 03 FF               JSR   CONOUT   
E03B   E8           PDROP:    INX      ; a drop for this page's handlers
E03C   E8                     INX      
E03D   60                     RTS      
E03E                             ; "#" prints TOS as a signed decimal number, with no space after it. The
E03E                             ; digits are divided off and held on the hardware stack, ended by a 0. udiv
E03E                             ; leaves each remainder in the scratch under the top cell.
E03E   B5 01        H_NUM:    LDA   1,X   
E040   10 08                  BPL   pn1   
E042   A9 2D                  LDA   #"-"   
E044   20 03 FF               JSR   CONOUT   
E047   20 2A E1               JSR   neg   
E04A   A9 0A        PN1:      LDA   #10   
E04C   85 08                  STA   D0   
E04E   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E050   85 09                  STA   D1   
E052   48                     PHA      ; the end marker
E053   20 EE E0     PN2:      JSR   udiv   
E056   B5 FE                  LDA   0FEh,X   
E058   09 30                  ORA   #"0"   
E05A   48                     PHA      
E05B   B5 00                  LDA   0,X   
E05D   15 01                  ORA   1,X   
E05F   D0 F2                  BNE   pn2   
E061   68           PN3:      PLA      
E062   F0 D7                  BEQ   pdrop   
E064   20 03 FF               JSR   CONOUT   
E067   4C 61 E0               JMP   pn3   
E06A                             ; "!" ( n addr -- ): the low byte, then "."s ending for the high byte.
E06A   B5 02        H_STORE:   LDA   2,X   
E06C                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JSR   chkst   
E06C                          ENDIF      
E06C   81 00                  STA   (0,X)   
E06E   20 A1 E2               JSR   inctos   
E071   B5 03                  LDA   3,X   
E073   4C 41 E1               JMP   st9   
E076   B5 00        H_SWAP:   LDA   0,X   ; "%"
E078   B4 02                  LDY   2,X   
E07A   94 00                  STY   0,X   
E07C   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E07E   B5 01                  LDA   1,X   
E080   B4 03                  LDY   3,X   
E082   94 01                  STY   1,X   
E084   95 03                  STA   3,X   
E086   60                     RTS      
E087                             ; "&", and "|" and "^" further down: one byte of the result goes into NOS,
E087                             ; and X moves up one. Done twice, that is both bytes, and NOS has become TOS.
E087   20 8A E0     H_AND:    JSR   an1   
E08A   B5 00        AN1:      LDA   0,X   
E08C   35 02                  AND   2,X   
E08E   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E090   E8                     INX      
E091   60                     RTS      
E092                             ; 22h pushes the address of the text after it and skips the string.
E092   20 58 E3     H_STR:    JSR   puship   
E095                             ; skipstr: IP is inside a string; step past its closing quote. "/" escapes
E095                             ; the next character, so "/"" does not end it. Returns with Z set, A = 22h.
E095   20 DD E3     SKIPSTR:   JSR   getc   
E098   C9 2F                  CMP   #"/"   
E09A   F0 05                  BEQ   ss0   
E09C   C9 22                  CMP   #22h   
E09E   D0 F5                  BNE   skipstr   
E0A0   60                     RTS      
E0A1   20 E0 E3     SS0:      JSR   incip   
E0A4   D0 EF                  BNE   skipstr   ; A is "/" here
E0A6                             ; "(" ( flag -- ): true pushes the block start and enters; false skips to
E0A6                             ; the matching ")".
E0A6   20 68 E3     H_OPEN:   JSR   flag   ; (keeps the carry clear)
E0A9   F0 17                  BEQ   skip   
E0AB                             ; pushn: take the step frame off, push the reading position on the return
E0AB                             ; stack, and go on. With the carry set (a word call) the reading position
E0AB                             ; then becomes the address in the letter cell at LET,Y.
E0AB   68           PUSHN:    PLA      
E0AC   68                     PLA      ; drop the step frame
E0AD   A5 01                  LDA   IP+1   
E0AF   48                     PHA      
E0B0   A5 00                  LDA   IP   
E0B2   48                     PHA      
E0B3   90 0A                  BCC   nxt   
E0B5   B9 00 04               LDA   LET,Y   
E0B8   85 00                  STA   IP   
E0BA   B9 01 04               LDA   LET+1,Y   
E0BD   85 01                  STA   IP+1   
E0BF   4C 1A E0     NXT:      JMP   next   
E0C2                             ; ---- no handler starts between here and the next heading: these routines
E0C2                             ; ---- fill the space up to the page boundary and past it ---------------------
E0C2                             ; skip: read past text to the closer at this level, and return it in A.
E0C2                             ; Brackets of both kinds count together; strings are stepped over whole. A
E0C2                             ; NUL first ends the program.
E0C2   A9 00        SKIP:     LDA   #0   
E0C4   85 06                  STA   T2   ; depth
E0C6   20 DD E3     SK1:      JSR   getc   
E0C9   F0 20                  BEQ   endprog   
E0CB   C9 22                  CMP   #22h   
E0CD   D0 03                  BNE   sk2   
E0CF   20 95 E0               JSR   skipstr   ; leaves A = 22h, which matches nothing below
E0D2   C9 28        SK2:      CMP   #"("   
E0D4   F0 11                  BEQ   sk3   
E0D6   C9 5B                  CMP   #"["   
E0D8   F0 0D                  BEQ   sk3   
E0DA   C9 29                  CMP   #")"   
E0DC   F0 04                  BEQ   sk4   
E0DE   C9 5D                  CMP   #"]"   
E0E0   D0 E4                  BNE   sk1   
E0E2   C6 06        SK4:      DEC   T2   
E0E4   10 E0                  BPL   sk1   
E0E6   60                     RTS      ; the closer at this level
E0E7   E6 06        SK3:      INC   T2   
E0E9   D0 DB                  BNE   sk1   
E0EB   4C 09 FF     ENDPROG:   JMP   EXIT   
E0EE                          IF   1-CHECKED   
E0EE                          ;*Macro unroll:  UDIV
E0EE   A9 00        UDIV:     LDA   #0   
E0F0   95 FE                  STA   0FEh,X   
E0F2   95 FF                  STA   0FFh,X   
E0F4   A5 08                  LDA   D0   
E0F6   49 02                  EOR   #2   
E0F8   05 09                  ORA   D1   
E0FA   D0 07                  BNE   ud0   
E0FC   56 01                  LSR   1,X   
E0FE   76 00                  ROR   0,X   
E100   36 FE                  ROL   0FEh,X   
E102   60                     RTS      
E103   A0 10        UD0:      LDY   #16   
E105   16 00        UD1:      ASL   0,X   
E107   36 01                  ROL   1,X   
E109   36 FE                  ROL   0FEh,X   
E10B   36 FF                  ROL   0FFh,X   
E10D   38                     SEC      
E10E   B5 FE                  LDA   0FEh,X   
E110   E5 08                  SBC   D0   
E112   85 04                  STA   T0   
E114   B5 FF                  LDA   0FFh,X   
E116   E5 09                  SBC   D1   
E118   90 08                  BCC   ud2   
E11A   95 FF                  STA   0FFh,X   
E11C   A5 04                  LDA   T0   
E11E   95 FE                  STA   0FEh,X   
E120   F6 00                  INC   0,X   
E122   88           UD2:      DEY      
E123   D0 E0                  BNE   ud1   
E125   60                     RTS      
E126                          ENDIF      
E126                             ; absv: TOS = |TOS|.  negm: TOS = -TOS if the N flag is set.
E126   B5 01        ABSV:     LDA   1,X   
E128   10 0D        NEGM:     BPL   ab9   
E12A                             ; neg: TOS = -TOS
E12A   38           NEG:      SEC      
E12B   A9 00        NEG1:     LDA   #0   
E12D   F5 00                  SBC   0,X   
E12F   95 00                  STA   0,X   
E131   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E133   F5 01                  SBC   1,X   
E135   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E137   60           AB9:      RTS      
E138                             ; =============================================================================
E138                             ;  Page 2 of 3: handlers of ) * + , - . / : ; < = > ?
E138                             ; =============================================================================
E138   B5 00        H_DUP:    LDA   0,X   ; ":"
E13A   B4 01                  LDY   1,X   
E13C   4C 61 E3               JMP   pushay   
E13F                             ; "." ( c addr -- ), and the end of "!": store A at TOS, drop both cells.
E13F   B5 02        H_CSTORE:   LDA   2,X   
E141                ST9:         
E141                          IF   CHECKED   
                              JSR   chkst   
E141                          ENDIF      
E141   81 00                  STA   (0,X)   
E143   E8                     INX      
E144   E8                     INX      
E145   E8           DROP:     INX      ; ";"
E146   E8                     INX      
E147   60                     RTS      
E148                             ; "-" has two jobs: directly before a digit it starts a negative number.
E148   20 C3 E3     H_SUB:    JSR   isdig   
E14B   90 0F                  BCC   negnum   
E14D   20 2A E1     SUB1:     JSR   neg   ; a - b = a + (-b)
E150   18                     CLC      
E151                             ; "+": one byte at a time, as the logic operators do; the carry goes across.
E151                             ; It ends with A = the high byte of the sum.
E151   20 54 E1     H_ADD:    JSR   ad1   
E154   B5 00        AD1:      LDA   0,X   
E156   75 02                  ADC   2,X   
E158   95 02                  STA   2,X   
E15A   E8                     INX      
E15B   60                     RTS      
E15C   A9 00        NEGNUM:   LDA   #0   ; a number from 0 (the digit routine's start)
E15E   20 8F E3               JSR   digit   ; (comes back with the carry set)
E161   B0 C7                  BCS   neg   ; always: the number, negated
E163                             ; Signed compares: a - b sets N and V; N xor V is "a < b". The carry ends up
E163                             ; clear for true, and bool turns it into -1 (true) or 0.
E163   20 76 E0     H_GT:     JSR   h_swap   ; a > b is b < a
E166   B5 02        H_LT:     LDA   2,X   
E168   D5 00                  CMP   0,X   
E16A   B5 03                  LDA   3,X   
E16C   F5 01                  SBC   1,X   
E16E   70 02                  BVS   lt1   
E170   49 80                  EOR   #80h   
E172   0A           LT1:      ASL      
E173   E8                     INX      
E174   E8                     INX      ; (keeps the carry; X is never 0)
E175   D0 07                  BNE   bool   ; always
E177   20 4D E1     H_EQ:     JSR   sub1   ; a - b: zero if equal; A = its high byte
E17A   15 00                  ORA   0,X   
E17C   C9 01                  CMP   #1   ; carry clear if A = 0
E17E   A9 00        BOOL:     LDA   #0   
E180   69 FF                  ADC   #0FFh   ; carry clear: FF, set: 00
E182   95 00                  STA   0,X   
E184   95 01                  STA   1,X   
E186   60                     RTS      
E187                             ; "*": the classic shift-right multiply. The multiplier is the NOS cell: it
E187                             ; leaves from the bottom while the product comes in at the top, through
E187                             ; A:T0 above it; after 16 rounds the cell holds the low 16 bits, which are
E187                             ; all that is kept. Dropping TOS (the multiplicand) leaves it on top.
E187   A0 10        H_MUL:    LDY   #16   ; the round count
E189   A9 00                  LDA   #0   
E18B   85 04                  STA   T0   ; A:T0 = the upper half
E18D   56 03                  LSR   3,X   
E18F   76 02                  ROR   2,X   ; the first multiplier bit
E191   90 0D        ML1:      BCC   ml2   
E193   85 05                  STA   T1   
E195   18                     CLC      
E196   A5 04                  LDA   T0   
E198   75 00                  ADC   0,X   
E19A   85 04                  STA   T0   
E19C   A5 05                  LDA   T1   
E19E   75 01                  ADC   1,X   ; upper half += the multiplicand
E1A0   6A           ML2:      ROR      
E1A1   66 04                  ROR   T0   
E1A3   76 03                  ROR   3,X   
E1A5   76 02                  ROR   2,X   ; everything one place right; next bit out
E1A7   88                     DEY      
E1A8   D0 E7                  BNE   ml1   
E1AA   E8                     INX      
E1AB   E8                     INX      
E1AC   60                     RTS      
E1AD                             ; "/" ( a b -- q r ): the remainder is on top. Truncates toward zero; r takes
E1AD                             ; a's sign. Divide |a| by |b|, then q gets the sign of a XOR b and r the sign
E1AD                             ; of a. Dividing by zero is not checked: it returns two cells of no meaning.
E1AD                             ; b's cell is dropped once D holds |b|; udiv then leaves the remainder just
E1AD                             ; where that cell was, so stepping X back down makes it the top cell.
E1AD   B5 03        H_DIV:    LDA   3,X   
E1AF   48                     PHA      ; a's sign: for r
E1B0   55 01                  EOR   1,X   
E1B2   48                     PHA      ; a xor b: for q
E1B3   20 26 E1               JSR   absv   
